+The harness also records why each arm lands where it does. Greedy finds a
+reusable prefix of 7.17 chunks out of ten on average, strictly positive on
+84.0\% of requests, and changes the retrieval order on 43.3\% of them: the
+win comes from a lever that is engaged often and deeply, not from a rare
+large gain. \texttt{pinned\_prefix} fails for a diagnosable reason rather
+than a mechanical one. Its pinning logic behaves exactly as designed --
+at top-$k{=}3$ it engages on 57.0\% of requests, matching the
+$0.75\times0.762=57.2\%$ predicted by session length four and the measured
+fraction of adjacent same-session pairs sharing at least one chunk -- yet
+at top-$k{=}10$ it never pins the whole list (0.0\% of requests) and pins
+3.38 chunks on average while still perturbing 65.7\% of orders. The result
+is a session-specific prefix in front of roughly seven fresh chunks, which
+suppresses precisely the cross-session sharing a prefix cache lives on.
+The design worked and the idea did not.
