+The signed error separates the two conventions more sharply than the
+correlation does, and in the direction the mechanism predicts. Dispatch-time
+recording is optimistic by construction -- a block is counted before prefill
+has run -- so it should overestimate, and it does at both loads
+($+31.3{\pm}9.2$ and $+23.7{\pm}1.0$ tokens), never once underestimating
+across six runs. Completion-time recording cannot see sibling requests still
+in flight, so it should underestimate more as concurrency rises, and it does:
+from $-5.2{\pm}20.3$ tokens at $5\times$ to $-170.3{\pm}25.5$ at $15\times$.
+That only one convention degrades, in its predicted direction, is what
+distinguishes this from load degrading both. This historical
+50{,}000-block sweep supports the relative load mechanism, not calibrated
+absolute error.
 
